During the Great Depression and World War II, the work of building cooperative housing, or any housing for that matter, mostly just stopped. Which is why, after World War II, New York, along with a lot of urban America, found itself in a pretty acute housing shortage. In 1949, the federal government passed the American Housing Act to help fix it. The new legislation had provisions to promote homeownership and the construction of public housing. And it also provided a bunch of money to clear blighted neighborhoods, or so-called slums. If we don't clean out these slums, the central areas are going to rock. There are very few cases where genuine slums, can be fixed up in any other way than by tearing them down.
在大萧条和第二次世界大战期间，建造合作住房或任何住房的工作基本上都停滞了。这就是为什么在二战之后，纽约以及许多美国城市发现自己面临着相当严重的住房短缺。1949年，联邦政府通过了《美国住房法》来帮助解决这一问题。新立法规定了促进房屋所有权和公共住房建设的条款。它还提供了一大笔资金来清理荒废的街区，也就是所谓的贫民窟。如果我们不清除这些贫民窟，中心区就会腐烂。极少数情况下，真正的贫民窟能够通过除了拆除之外的其他任何方式得到修复。

