and it stopped providing certain kinds of maintenance to the buildings. The residents would have to do some things themselves. People were sort of washing the floor. in the halls of their own building and the public halls of their own buildings. You know, I think my mother chipped in, to help mock the public hall floors. This is Noel Ellison.
并且停止为大楼提供某些类型的维护。居民们不得不自己做一些事情。人们开始在自己大楼的走廊和大楼的公共大厅里清洗地板。你知道，我想我母亲也出了力，帮忙擦洗公共大厅的地板。这是诺埃尔·埃里森。

47
00:27:41.000 --> 00:28:09.000
He's lived in Co-op City since the days of the strike. It kind of forced you to get to know your neighbors as well. I think I've got a picture of my mother sitting in a meeting with other wives or people from the floor. Noel remembers it all feeling really well-organized. which makes sense because a lot of the residents of Co-op City belonged to unions. They knew how to run a strike. The strike lasted 13 months.
自从罢工的日子以来，他就一直住在合作城。这倒也逼着你去了解自己的邻居。我想我有一张照片，拍的是我母亲和其他几位妻子或同楼层的人坐在一起开会。诺尔记得这一切感觉组织得非常好，这很合理，因为合作城的许多居民都属于工会。他们知道如何组织罢工。这场罢工持续了13个月。

48
00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:44.000
In the end, the state agreed to help with some large repairs that were needed on the buildings. But the residents didn't get any significant relief on their mortgage. They did, however, get control of Co-op City. The United Housing Foundation was out. After the residents took over Co-op City in 1976, the UHF would not. never build another cooperative. You could say that, like, there was never a greater demonstration of the cooperative spirit than the Wrenstrike that destroyed the United's Housing Foundation.
最后，州政府同意协助进行建筑物所需的一些重大维修。但居民们的按揭贷款并没有得到实质性的减负。不过，他们确实获得了对合作城的控制权。联合住房基金会出局了。在居民于1976年接管合作城之后，联合住房基金会再也没有建造过任何其他的合作公寓。你可以说，就像雷恩罢工摧毁了联合住房基金会那样，再也没有比这更能体现合作精神的壮举了。

49
00:28:44.000 --> 00:29:14.000
Co-op City itself was the cragmire from which the movement could never escape. That was it. It wasn't only that Co-op City had been a disaster for the UHF. By the mid-1970s, the project of big government liberalism was over, and a small government neoliberal era had begun. Abraham Kaysen had died. Robert Moses was more or less retired. And although many of the existing Mitchell Lama developments in New York
合作城本身就是那个运动永远无法逃脱的泥潭。就是这样。不仅因为合作城对联合住房基金会来说是一场灾难。到20世纪70年代中期，大政府自由主义的项目已经结束，小政府的新自由主义时代开始了。亚伯拉罕·凯森已经去世。罗伯特·摩西也差不多退休了。尽管纽约现有的许多米切尔-拉马开发项目

50
00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:40.000
would continue to receive government subsidies, nothing new would be built under the program after the late 70s. Mitchell Lama was not the only thing to lose funding in the 1970s. A worldwide recession meant that it was a period of austerity for the whole country. and certainly for New York. The city pulled back on essential services like police and firefighters. Unemployment and crime went up. People who could leave the city did,
将继续获得政府补贴，但在70年代末之后，该计划下将不会再建设任何新项目。米切尔-拉马并不是在20世纪70年代唯一失去资金的东西。全球经济衰退意味着整个国家都处于紧缩时期，纽约当然也不例外。市政府削减了警察和消防员等基本服务。失业率和犯罪率上升了。能离开这座城市的人都离开了，

51
00:29:40.000 --> 00:30:17.000
and the white flight that Robert Moses had tried to stave off finally came knocking. Co-op city was no exception. Early residents had been about 80% white, the vast majority of them Jewish, and about 20% people of color. This had been similar to the racial makeup of the Bronx at the time. But by the mid-1970s, the Bronx was changing, and so was Co-op City. By 1976, 90% of the people on the waiting list to move into Co-op City are black and Hispanic.
罗伯特·摩西一直试图阻止的白人群飞终于敲响了门。合作城也不例外。早期的居民大约80%是白人，其中绝大多数是犹太人，大约20%是有色人种。这与当时布朗克斯区的种族构成相似。但到20世纪70年代中期，布朗克斯区正在改变，合作城也是如此。到1976年，等待搬入合作城的人中有90%是黑人和西班牙裔。

52
00:30:17.000 --> 00:31:02.000
Co-op City goes from being a majority white development to being one that is not, really over the course of the 1980s. While the Orthodox Synagogue on the grounds of Co-op City scaled back services for lack of congregants, The cooperative's own Harry S. Truman High School began offering a class on African American studies, and the development became a hub of early hip-hop culture. In a 1986 documentary about rap in New York City called Big Fun in the Big Town, the filmmakers interviewed a group of students from Harry S. Truman High on the grounds of Coop City. You can see the skyscrapers in the background. Here we are for fun. Guaranteed to make it any party line. So just when it's your air.
在20世纪80年代的进程中，合作城从一个白人占多数的开发区转变为一个并非如此的开发区。当合作城内的正统犹太会堂因缺少会众而缩减服务时，该合作社自己的哈里·S·杜鲁门高中开始开设非洲裔美国人研究课程，该开发区也成为了早期嘻哈文化的中心。在1986年一部关于纽约市说唱的纪录片《大镇里的巨乐》中，电影制片人在合作城的土地上采访了一群来自哈里·S·杜鲁门高中的学生。你可以看到背景中的摩天大楼。我们是为了好玩而来的。保证让它成为任何派对的热点。所以当你呼吸空气时。

53
00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:40.000
If you please. And if you don't want to rock, then you might as well leave. What Co-op City was undergoing in the 1980s was indeed a racial transition. And I remember this very clearly growing up. Like when I was in eighth grade, all these families moved out. Anne-Marie says that with this racial transition came some anxiety about crime and what would become of this middle-class development. There's all these anxieties about co-op city's supposed decline, anxieties that were voiced by white residents of Co-op City for sure, but also by Black residents of Co-op City.
如果你愿意的话。如果你不想摇滚，那么你最好离开。合作城在20世纪80年代所经历的确实是一次种族转型。我在成长过程中对此记忆犹新。就像我上八年级的时候，所有这些家庭都搬走了。安妮-玛丽说，伴随着这种种族转型而来的，是对犯罪以及这个中产阶级开发项目将会变成什么的某种焦虑。对合作城所谓衰落的各种焦虑不绝于耳，这些焦虑当然是由合作城的白人居民表达的，但也是由合作城的黑人居民表达的。

54
00:31:40.000 --> 00:32:17.000
And crime did go up some in the 1980s, as it did all over New York City. But a lot of this decline that people worried about at Co-op City, Ann Marie says it just never really came to pass. You know, often when you talk about neighborhoods that undergo, like, racial change, you know, where there's white flight, It's either a story of violence or a story of, you know, neglect or whatever. And Co-op City, it's not really that story. Co-op City did become a majority black community by 1990. It has remained that way to this day. But it has stayed at the same time a middle-class community.
在20世纪80年代，犯罪率确实有所上升，就像整个纽约市一样。但安妮-玛丽说，人们所担心的合作城的这种衰落，很多其实并没有真正发生。你知道，通常当你谈到经历种族变化的社区时，你知道，有白人群飞的地方，要么是一个关于暴力或疏忽的故事，诸如此类。而合作城，其实并不是那样的故事。到1990年，合作城确实成为了一个以黑人为主的社区。它一直保持这种状态直到今天。但与此同时，它依然是一个中产阶级社区。

55
00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:50.000
Emory says that Co-op City's ability to stay middle-class, even as it went through a big racial transition, may have had something to do with that equity deposit. the thing that the UHF had always been so adamant about. In the early years, that deposit had been a barrier to people of color. But by the mid-1970s, the black middle class had grown, and more families could afford the upfront investment. And that investment may have helped create some stability during a decade when much of the Bronx was emptying out. It's harder to just pick up and leave when you have to sell your share
埃默里表示，合作城能够保持中产阶级属性，甚至在经历大规模种族转型时也是如此，这可能与那个股权押金有关，这也是联合住房基金会一直坚持的一点。在早期，这笔押金曾是有色人种的门槛。但到了20世纪70年代中期，黑人中产阶级已经壮大，更多的家庭能够负担得起前期投资。这种投资可能在20世纪70年代布朗克斯区大面积空心化期间带来了一些稳定性。当你必须卖掉自己的股份时，想要直接拍屁股走人就没那么容易了

