Like, that's a long time to live in a place. Yeah, absolutely. You know, they're somewhere between a tenant and an owner, you know, but I think the right word is like investment, you know, not just financial, but like a investment in a stake in a community that a typical New York tenant did not have. I don't want my parents to go anywhere. I want them to stay right in the apartment because it's affordable. And, you know, we can have health care attendants come in. And I mean, it's an ideal place still for someone of their income bracket and their stage of life to live. Frank's parents are part of a huge constituency of older residents who currently live in Co-op City. In fact, the development has become the largest naturally occurring retirement community, or Nork, in the U.S., partly because it's an affordable place to live on a fixed income in a very expensive city. There was a period during and after the rent strike when Co-op City was looked at as a failure.
比如，在一个地方住那么久是很不容易的。是的，绝对的。你知道，他们介于租户和业主之间，你知道，但我认为合适的词就像是投资，你知道的，不仅仅是财务上的，而是一种对社区权益的投资，这是典型的纽约租户所没有的。我不想让我父母去任何地方。我希望他们就留在公寓里，因为这里价格实惠。而且，你知道，我们可以请保健护工来。我的意思是，对于他们这个收入阶层和人生阶段的人来说，这里仍然是一个理想的居住地。弗兰克的父母是目前居住在合作城的庞大老年居民群体的一部分。事实上，该开发项目已经成为美国最大的自然发生的退休社区（Nork），部分原因是在一个非常昂贵的城市里，这是一个靠固定收入就能负担得起的地方。在租金罢工期间及之后的一段时间里，合作城曾被视为一个失败的案例。

58
00:34:19.000 --> 00:35:07.000
The state had subsidized the building of this massive and many-thought, ugly cluster of skyscrapers. And then its residents refused for over a year to pay their own mortgage. There were politicians and pundits during the strike that suggested that government should foreclose on the mortgage and evict the residents. Walk away from this expensive project and cut its losses. And it's true that the development has needed continued subsidies from the state over the years. Building on top of a swamp has meant ongoing structural issues that residents have not been able to afford on their own. But if co-op city once served as a cautionary tale about the perils of big government ambition, it now stands as a reminder of what that kind of ambition can create.
州政府曾补贴建设了这个庞大的、许多人认为丑陋的摩天大楼群。然后，居民们却有一年多的时间拒绝支付自己的房贷。罢工期间，有政客和专家建议政府取消抵押贷款赎回权并驱逐居民。放弃这个昂贵的项目并止损。的确，多年来该开发项目一直需要州政府的持续补贴。建立在沼泽地上意味着居民无力自行承担的持续结构性问题。但是，如果合作城曾一度成为大政府雄心弊端的警世故事，那么它现在则是这种雄心能够创造什么的纪念碑。

59
00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:48.000
Speaking of ambition, the new mayor of New York, Zoran Mamdani, has a plan to build 200,000 units of a portable housing over 10 years. It's a goal that former mayor Bill de Blasio also had but failed to accomplish. It won't be easy. The last time anyone built housing on this scale in New York City, well, you just heard the story of it. It was after World War II, spearheaded by people like Robert Moses and Abraham Kaysen. They made a lot of extremely harmful mistakes along the way, bulldozing neighborhoods, displacing residents, treating whole community. like they were expendable.
说到雄心，纽约新市长佐兰·马姆达尼有一个计划，要在10年内建设20万套经济适用房。这是前市长比尔·德布拉西奥也有过但未能实现的目标。这并不容易。上一次有人在纽约市以这种规模建造住房时，你刚刚听过它的故事。那是在二战之后，由罗伯特·摩西和亚伯拉罕·凯森等人带头。他们在过程中犯了许多极具破坏性的错误，推平社区，流离失所居民，把整个社区当成可以牺牲的弃子。

60
00:35:48.000 --> 00:36:30.000
But the ambition to solve a housing crisis wasn't a mistake. And in a city once again wrestling with how to house the people who keep it running, the question isn't whether the government should attempt something that big again. It's whether it can afford not to. 99% Invisible was produced this week by Katie Minkle and edited by Christopher Johnson. This episode was mixed by Martine Gonzalez with music by Swan Real and George Langford. Fact-checking by Graham Hesha. Some audio from this episode came from the municipal archives in New York City and the oral history archives at Columbia.
